# AMD GPU ISA

$n_instructions instructions, $n_encodings encodings, $n_operands operands across
$n_archs architectures, in a $db_size SQLite database. Built $built from
$source_desc.

**Query with `scripts/isa.py`. Never `Read` the database.** `$S` below = the
directory containing this SKILL.md — resolve it from this file's location, the
install path differs per agent.

```bash
python3 $S/scripts/isa.py --help
```

There is no `sqlite3` CLI dependency — `isa.py` uses Python's stdlib, so it runs
anywhere python3 does.

## Decision table

| question | command |
|---|---|
| everything about one instruction | `isa.py show V_FMA_F32 --arch rdna4` |
| which archs have it, opcode per arch | `isa.py which V_DOT2_F32_BF16` |
| find instructions by pattern | `isa.py search '^v_mfma.*f16$' [--arch A] [--group G]` |
| search descriptions, not names | `isa.py search --fts 'dot AND bf16'` |
| everything in a group / encoding | `isa.py list --arch cdna5 --group VALU` |
| microcode bit layout of an encoding | `isa.py encodings --arch rdna4 --encoding ENC_VOP3` |
| all encodings in an arch | `isa.py encodings --arch rdna4` |
| what changed between two archs | `isa.py diff cdna4 cdna5 [--group SMEM]` |
| arch / group overview | `isa.py archs`, `isa.py groups --arch cdna5` |
| anything else | `isa.py sql "<SELECT ...>"` |
| verify the corpus answers correctly | `isa.py selftest` ($n_selftests examples) |

Every subcommand takes `--json` and `-n N` (`-n 0` = unlimited). **The true row
count is always reported**, so a truncated listing never reads as a complete
answer. Exit 1 means no match, and always suggests a next command.

Aliases resolve automatically: `isa.py show S_LOAD_DWORD` finds `S_LOAD_B32` and
says so. Names drift between generations and between LLVM and the manuals — trust
`which`, not memory.

## Writing SQL

When the subcommands do not fit, query directly. `isa.py schema` prints the full
schema; these flattened views answer most questions with no join:

```
v_inst      arch, name, "group", encoding, opcode, condition, description
v_presence  arch, name, "group", n_encodings, opcode      -- one row per (name, arch)
v_name      arch, query_name, canonical_name, is_alias    -- alias resolution
v_operand   arch, name, encoding, ord, field_name, operand_type, data_format,
            size, is_input, is_output, is_implicit
```

```bash
isa.py sql "SELECT arch, COUNT(*) FROM v_presence WHERE \"group\"='VALU' GROUP BY arch"
isa.py sql "SELECT name FROM v_presence WHERE arch='cdna5'
            EXCEPT SELECT name FROM v_presence WHERE arch='cdna4'"
```

Only `SELECT`/`WITH` are accepted and the connection is read-only, so a query
cannot damage the corpus. Base tables — `instruction`, `inst_encoding`,
`operand`, `encoding`, `encoding_field`, `data_format`, `operand_type`,
`functional_group`, `arch` — are there when a view is not enough.

## Known limits

- **Structure only, no semantics.** The XML carries names, encodings, opcodes,
  operands and one-line descriptions. It has no pseudocode and no prose.
  $enrich_status
- **No gfx target mapping.** The XML names no gfx targets, so "is gfx950 CDNA4"
  cannot be answered from this corpus. Check `llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/GCNProcessors.td`
  in an LLVM checkout.
- **Not cross-checked against LLVM.** Where this corpus and
  `llvm/lib/Target/AMDGPU/*.td` disagree, LLVM is what the compiler actually
  does; this is what AMD published.

